    "title": "Artemis II splashdown time: NASA return schedule April 10",
    "text": "IN SPACE - APRIL 03: (EDITOR'S NOTE: This Handout image was provided by a third-party organization and may not adhere to Getty Images' editorial policy.) In this handout image provided by NASA, Orion snapped this high-resolution selfie in space with a camera mounted on one of its solar array wings during a routine external inspection of the spacecraft on the second day into the Artemis II mission on April 3, 2026. NASA's Artemis II mission will take Wiseman, Glover, Koch, and Hansen on a 10-day journey around the Moon and back aboard their Orion spacecraft. (Photo by NASA via Getty Images)NASA/NASA via Getty ImagesNASA’s Artemis II crew has completed its primary mission objectives and is now preparing to return to Earth later this week.The Artemis II mission set a new record for the farthest human spaceflight at 6:07 p.m. CT on April 6, 2026, reaching 252,760 miles from Earth. The crew broke the previous record of 248,655 miles set by Apollo 13 earlier that day at 12:56 p.m. CT.Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢“It is blowing my mind what you can see with the naked eye from the moon right now. It is just unbelievable,” Canadian astronaut Jeremy Hansen radioed. He added that future missions should ensure “this record is not long-lived.”The 10-day mission will conclude April 10 with a planned splashdown off the California coast. NASA expects the crew to land in the Pacific Ocean near San Diego at 5:07 p.m. PDT (7:07 p.m. CT) Friday.Here’s what to know about the return schedule.When does Artemis II return to Earth?Artemis II’s lunar flyby and observation period lasted about seven hours, a key milestone in the nearly 10-day test flight. The mission will end with a splashdown Friday, April 10, in the Pacific Ocean.Article continues below this adNASA says the spacecraft is following a “free-return” lunar trajectory — a path that uses the gravity of Earth and the moon to guide Orion back while minimizing fuel use.Here is the planned timeline for April 10 (all times CT):Article continues below this ad 2:05 a.m.: Crew sleep begins 10:35 a.m.: Flight Day 10 crew wakeup 12:50 p.m.: Orion cabin configuration for re-entry begins 1:53 p.m.: Return trajectory correction burn 5:30 p.m.: NASA+ coverage begins 6:33 p.m.: Crew module and service module separation 6:37 p.m.: Crew module raise burn 6:53 p.m.: Entry interface 7:07 p.m.: Splashdown in the Pacific Ocean 9:30 p.m.: Post-splashdown news conference at NASA Johnson Space Center Stories you won't want to miss, handpicked by Statesman editors.April 7, 2026Marley Malenfant is a 10-year multimedia journalist who’s worked in print, digital, and broadcast. His prior work includes working in West Texas as a TV reporter for KTXS. During his time there, he covered education, sports, features, and breaking News. He’s skilled in front of the camera as well as behind it. He was a freelance writer, published in D Magazine, Dallas Morning News, SB Nation, Dallas Observer, and was a personality for “The Coach Brothers” sports radio show. Outside of journalism, he was an educator in the Dallas area. He was a 2012 Mayborn Literary Nonfiction Conference grant recipient. In 2021, he was part of the NABJ student multimedia project. He graduated with a paralegal degree from El Centro in 2023. Currently, he’s a trending news reporter for the Austin American-Statesman, covering breaking News, sports, music, and legal trends.",
